1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,760
A waiter holds a tray on one hand.

2
00:00:02,760 --> 00:00:05,156
A tightrope walker grips a long pole.

3
00:00:05,156 --> 00:00:07,419
A crane lifts a load by one hook.

4
00:00:07,819 --> 00:00:11,678
Each one finds a special point,
where all the mass seems to sit.

5
00:00:12,128 --> 00:00:15,609
Diya balances her thirty
centimetre ruler on one finger.

6
00:00:15,609 --> 00:00:17,752
It balances right at the middle.

7
00:00:18,102 --> 00:00:20,646
Then she stacks four coins on one end.

8
00:00:20,646 --> 00:00:22,135
Now the middle fails.

9
00:00:22,135 --> 00:00:24,572
Where does it balance now, and why?

10
00:00:25,072 --> 00:00:28,083
The answer is a point
called the centre of mass.

11
00:00:28,683 --> 00:00:33,260
The centre of mass is the average
position of all the mass in a body.

12
00:00:33,610 --> 00:00:35,081
Not a plain average.

13
00:00:35,081 --> 00:00:39,811
Each bit counts by its mass, so heavy
bits pull the average toward them.

14
00:00:40,211 --> 00:00:43,002
A ruler alone has its mass spread evenly.

15
00:00:43,002 --> 00:00:47,551
So its centre of mass is at the
middle, the fifteen centimetre mark.

16
00:00:48,151 --> 00:00:49,547
Let's make it simple.

17
00:00:49,547 --> 00:00:53,755
A light rod, one point two metres
long, with a ball at each end.

18
00:00:54,105 --> 00:00:57,496
Ball one, one kilogram,
sits at x equals zero.

19
00:00:57,496 --> 00:01:00,207
Ball two sits at one point two metres.

20
00:01:00,607 --> 00:01:04,817
With one kilogram each, the centre
of mass is right in the middle.

21
00:01:04,817 --> 00:01:06,328
Zero point six metres.

22
00:01:06,728 --> 00:01:08,410
Now make ball two heavier.

23
00:01:08,410 --> 00:01:09,383
Two kilograms.

24
00:01:09,383 --> 00:01:12,194
The centre of mass slides
to zero point eight.

25
00:01:12,594 --> 00:01:13,698
Three kilograms.

26
00:01:13,698 --> 00:01:15,642
It slides to zero point nine.

27
00:01:15,642 --> 00:01:17,625
Always toward the heavier ball.

28
00:01:18,125 --> 00:01:22,297
On the graph, the dot climbs as ball
two gets heavier, but it never passes

29
00:01:22,297 --> 00:01:23,269
one point two.

30
00:01:23,869 --> 00:01:25,929
Let's freeze at three kilograms.

31
00:01:26,279 --> 00:01:29,659
Ball one is zero point nine
metres from the centre.

32
00:01:29,659 --> 00:01:32,015
Ball two is only zero point three.

33
00:01:32,415 --> 00:01:35,419
Three times the mass,
one third the distance.

34
00:01:35,419 --> 00:01:39,330
One times zero point nine equals
three times zero point three.

35
00:01:39,930 --> 00:01:40,936
Quick check.

36
00:01:40,936 --> 00:01:44,077
Double both balls, to
two and six kilograms.

37
00:01:44,077 --> 00:01:46,213
Does the centre of mass move?

38
00:01:46,213 --> 00:01:47,541
Pause and decide.

39
00:01:50,641 --> 00:01:51,541
No!

40
00:01:51,039 --> 00:01:53,006
The ratio is still one to three.

41
00:01:53,006 --> 00:01:56,350
Only the ratio of the masses
decides where the centre sits.

42
00:01:56,950 --> 00:01:59,379
Let's write the rule, one step at a time.

43
00:01:59,729 --> 00:02:00,673
Step one.

44
00:02:00,673 --> 00:02:03,218
Count each position by its mass.

45
00:02:03,218 --> 00:02:08,779
X equals m one x one, plus m two
x two, all over m one plus m two.

46
00:02:09,529 --> 00:02:10,429
Step two.

47
00:02:10,349 --> 00:02:11,578
Check the units.

48
00:02:11,578 --> 00:02:14,386
Kilogram metres over
kilograms gives metres.

49
00:02:14,386 --> 00:02:15,885
So X is a position.

50
00:02:16,635 --> 00:02:17,647
Step three.

51
00:02:17,647 --> 00:02:18,769
Equal masses.

52
00:02:18,769 --> 00:02:22,928
The m cancels, and X is x
one plus x two, over two.

53
00:02:22,928 --> 00:02:24,050
The midpoint.

54
00:02:24,800 --> 00:02:25,716
Step four.

55
00:02:25,716 --> 00:02:29,800
In general, m one times r
one equals m two times r two.

56
00:02:29,800 --> 00:02:33,053
So the centre sits closer
to the heavier mass.

57
00:02:33,803 --> 00:02:38,164
For many particles, add every m
x, and divide by the total mass.

58
00:02:38,164 --> 00:02:39,129
Same for y.

59
00:02:39,729 --> 00:02:40,753
Remember this.

60
00:02:40,753 --> 00:02:45,444
The centre of mass is the average position
of the mass, closer to the heavier

61
00:02:45,444 --> 00:02:46,344
part.

62
00:02:46,680 --> 00:02:48,539
Now Diya's ruler, link by link.

63
00:02:48,889 --> 00:02:53,004
Gravity pulls on every coin,
and on every bit of the ruler.

64
00:02:53,404 --> 00:02:57,044
Near the Earth, each bit is
pulled in proportion to its mass.

65
00:02:57,444 --> 00:03:00,320
So the pulls balance at
the mass average point.

66
00:03:00,320 --> 00:03:02,821
The balance point is the centre of mass.

67
00:03:03,321 --> 00:03:08,033
Say the ruler is twenty grams, with four
five gram coins at the one centimetre

68
00:03:08,033 --> 00:03:08,933
mark.

69
00:03:08,566 --> 00:03:10,660
It balances at eight centimetres.

70
00:03:11,260 --> 00:03:12,350
Side by side.

71
00:03:12,350 --> 00:03:15,882
Equal balls: the centre of
mass sits in the middle.

72
00:03:16,282 --> 00:03:20,565
One and three kilograms: it sits
three quarters of the way across, near

73
00:03:20,565 --> 00:03:21,669
the heavy ball.

74
00:03:22,269 --> 00:03:23,237
Now you try.

75
00:03:23,237 --> 00:03:26,489
Put the zero of the scale
at the other end instead.

76
00:03:26,489 --> 00:03:28,425
Does the centre of mass move?

77
00:03:28,425 --> 00:03:29,628
Pause and decide.

78
00:03:32,828 --> 00:03:33,728
No!

79
00:03:33,255 --> 00:03:37,654
Every number changes, but the point
stays at the same place on the rod.

80
00:03:38,304 --> 00:03:39,995
Now, one worked example.

81
00:03:40,345 --> 00:03:42,328
One kilogram at the origin.

82
00:03:42,328 --> 00:03:44,853
Two kilograms at three metres on x.

83
00:03:44,853 --> 00:03:47,431
Three kilograms at four metres on y.

84
00:03:47,431 --> 00:03:49,258
Find the centre of mass.

85
00:03:49,758 --> 00:03:52,497
Total mass: one plus two plus three.

86
00:03:52,497 --> 00:03:53,637
Six kilograms.

87
00:03:54,387 --> 00:03:58,809
X: one times zero, plus two times
three, plus three times zero.

88
00:03:58,809 --> 00:04:00,039
Six, over six.

89
00:04:00,039 --> 00:04:00,939
One metre.

90
00:04:01,659 --> 00:04:05,305
Y: only the three kilogram
mass is off the x axis.

91
00:04:05,305 --> 00:04:08,026
Three times four is twelve, over six.

92
00:04:08,026 --> 00:04:08,981
Two metres.

93
00:04:09,731 --> 00:04:12,393
So the centre of mass is at one, two.

94
00:04:12,393 --> 00:04:15,589
It lies inside the triangle
the three masses make.

95
00:04:16,239 --> 00:04:17,700
Now, the exam trap.

96
00:04:18,050 --> 00:04:21,948
Many students put the centre of mass
halfway between two bodies, every

97
00:04:21,948 --> 00:04:22,848
time.

98
00:04:22,847 --> 00:04:25,254
That works only for equal masses.

99
00:04:25,254 --> 00:04:30,216
Otherwise it sits closer to the
heavier one: m one r one equals m two r

100
00:04:30,216 --> 00:04:31,116
two.

101
00:04:31,409 --> 00:04:32,932
Where is this asked?

102
00:04:32,932 --> 00:04:37,614
In two and three particle problems,
and in how far the centre shifts.

103
00:04:38,214 --> 00:04:39,313
Exam level now.

104
00:04:39,313 --> 00:04:43,885
Two particles, two kilograms and six
kilograms, are forty centimetres apart.

105
00:04:43,885 --> 00:04:48,140
The two kilogram one moves twelve
centimetres toward the centre of mass.

106
00:04:48,140 --> 00:04:51,977
How must the six kilogram one
move, to keep the centre fixed?

107
00:04:51,977 --> 00:04:53,122
Pause and solve.

108
00:04:58,522 --> 00:04:59,574
The answer is A.

109
00:04:59,574 --> 00:05:01,996
Four centimetres, toward
the centre of mass.

110
00:05:02,496 --> 00:05:04,050
X must not change.

111
00:05:04,050 --> 00:05:08,957
So m one times its shift, plus
m two times its shift, is zero.

112
00:05:09,457 --> 00:05:11,597
Two times twelve is twenty four.

113
00:05:11,597 --> 00:05:15,645
So six times the shift is twenty
four, and the shift is four.

114
00:05:16,145 --> 00:05:18,216
The two shifts point
in opposite directions.

115
00:05:18,216 --> 00:05:20,180
So both particles move toward the centre.

116
00:05:20,780 --> 00:05:22,320
Here is your recap.

117
00:05:22,320 --> 00:05:23,903
Screenshot this card.

118
00:05:27,103 --> 00:05:29,041
Can you answer these without help?

119
00:05:29,441 --> 00:05:30,341
One.

120
00:05:29,855 --> 00:05:33,035
Why do coins on one end move
the balance point toward them?

121
00:05:33,935 --> 00:05:34,835
Two.

122
00:05:34,396 --> 00:05:37,331
Where is the centre of
mass of two equal masses?

123
00:05:38,231 --> 00:05:39,131
Three.

124
00:05:38,768 --> 00:05:42,031
Does choosing a different
origin move the centre of mass?

125
00:05:42,931 --> 00:05:46,873
Next time: a ring balances right at
its centre, where there is no metal

126
00:05:46,873 --> 00:05:47,773
at all.

127
00:05:47,485 --> 00:05:48,385
How?

128
00:05:47,918 --> 00:05:49,796
Centre of mass of common bodies.
